<Layout title="Arraigo in Spain: Two-Year Rule | López Márquez Abogados" description="Current high-level information on the residence threshold relevant to some forms of arraigo and why the exact route still needs individual review.">
<PageHero eyebrow="Resource" title="Does Arraigo in Spain Still Require Three Years?" description="Under the current framework, certain forms of arraigo linked to residence use a two-year threshold. The applicant must still satisfy the additional requirements of the specific route." primaryLabel="Contact the firm" primaryHref="/en/contact" secondaryLabel="More resources" secondaryHref="/en/resources" note="General information · Check the current official source before acting" />
<section class="section"><div class="container prose"><h2>Two years is a threshold, not an automatic right</h2><p>For forms of arraigo that require a period of residence, two years is a relevant threshold under the current framework. The particular route and its additional requirements still have to be checked.</p><h2>What else needs to be reviewed?</h2><ul><li>How the residence in Spain can be evidenced.</li><li>The applicant’s current personal and administrative situation.</li><li>The specific family, employment or other requirement of the route.</li><li>Any notifications or previous applications.</li></ul><p><a href="https://www.boe.es/eli/es/rd/2024/11/19/1155/con" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the consolidated Immigration Regulation at the BOE →</a></p></div></section>
